#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221087 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b4d9d68-59b6-5209-8265-e1e062c422bf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "e9855de8-af52-5bc0-aae2-1dbc5906a659" :question/math-academy-id "e-21179" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/b6c879d367709165835c2de7cec94895897be567a9e264222506d4b2a63874e0.png)\nFind the Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2)$ of the above vector-valued functions." :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/ebce3767dfd869aa1ebf4bb03b9eb06a7f41dbb4b927a7699982f35d3a43a562.png)\nis defined as\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/b07428ffa056ae11d8fef349d666abab80e64bfc1822006f42db42a4b21e596d.png)\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3} & {t}^{3}-5 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={t}^{3}\\cdot 4-({t}^{3}-5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{3}+10 \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221088 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee461d3e-3afa-51c6-bddd-355668cac3ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "9ef2af54-fc18-5908-9adb-fb3e2d92c098" :question/math-academy-id "e-21180" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/235991f16a48086f1629a0519b1358db70ee0b26062a651a2a8adc305c682681.png)\nConsider the vector-valued functions $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ above. Given that $\\mathbf x_1$ and $\\mathbf x_2$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, compute the Wronskian is $W(\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2)$ and determine if the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent on $\\mathbb R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/51774bbcf79f8ab11069a5d7c955f73cd637f8407aa7a600673de0eaf9adb7ea.png)\nis defined as\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/ed9d2deba98ee27ebe8a183cab45f42a92f2d4f49733fa8a8f7c9acdf6185912.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{2t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-4{e}^{2t})\\cdot (2{e}^{t})-{e}^{t}\\cdot ({e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =-8{e}^{3t}-{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =-9{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in \\mathbb R.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\right\\}$ is linearly independent on $\\mathbb R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221089 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac674e3f-ac2b-5512-b4fa-9840c6cbd034" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions to Systems of Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "79080694-1e56-57e5-8249-9aadbcea4346" :question/math-academy-id "e-21181" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-4{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in\\mathbb R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/74e10d18890ebd27b2339e13ac2bbc9e0821c1dfea752478a90877d0a0059ff6.png) is a solution of the system.\n\n- The function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/f08f12db6684778c1f5734c0731a370663fcfb23cd958dde49e3a2f41cd0e50a.png) is a solution of the system.\n\n- The function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/f5c546b729e994f79408b4344979446aac8aa3820c1798251bc50378f8f7159b.png) is a solution of the system.\n\nAlso, determine which pair of solutions forms a set of fundamental solutions to the system." :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/e61be508e4ad25ed39996a7a924a732290f3c8be7e5e953e01da6ff35c5669f4.png)\nis defined as\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/ed9d2deba98ee27ebe8a183cab45f42a92f2d4f49733fa8a8f7c9acdf6185912.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/3ac4270c91e71468086149ed819c644c71d59aea898520acc8e5fbf30205985c.png)\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/7d502d9256ace32899ea72f143f3694db8f0531efebf2dbd0c20dc857bd563b5.png) $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({t}^{2}+1)^{′} \\\\ (-4t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -4t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)+1\\cdot (-4t) \\\\ -4\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)+(-2)\\cdot (-4t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}-4t+2 \\\\ -4{t}^{2}+8t-4\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/c223cfc85f5a1f23006dd9de1775f898731a2cce2b88d1c27c0ab3f4ee238e66.png) $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(2t+1)^{′} \\\\ (-4t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2t+1 \\\\ -4t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (2t+1)+1\\cdot (-4t) \\\\ -4\\cdot (2t+1)+(-2)\\cdot (-4t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/2f5d5dd7f1fe1b1a51df2930d780fc11af9a31f8cac9cc9c38f966eb42bc848d.png) $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(t)^{′} \\\\ (1-2t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1-2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot t+1\\cdot (1-2t) \\\\ -4\\cdot t+(-2)\\cdot (1-2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{b},\\mathbf{c} \\big\\}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(b,c) & =|\\begin{aligned}2t+1 & t \\\\ -4t & 1-2t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\cdot (1-2t)-t\\cdot (-4t) \\\\  & =(2t+1)(1-2t)+4{t}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t \\in \\mathbb R.$ Thus, since $\\mathbf b$ and $\\mathbf c$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{b},\\mathbf{c} \\big\\}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b4d9d68-59b6-5209-8265-e1e062c422bf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "230acb2e-2d20-5198-91a6-b3f7968c51b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "230acb2e-2d20-5198-91a6-b3f7968c51b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352366} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e593968-af4d-5666-af75-3cb617187b66" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c55f9ab8-e7b6-5ec8-9696-dd445096cc3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-322731" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(3t) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac674e3f-ac2b-5512-b4fa-9840c6cbd034" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions to Systems of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0211048-1573-5a10-9aca-ccfb8d67c24d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220062} {:answer/id #uuid "2a830313-9639-587a-b48c-d45715662c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a830313-9639-587a-b48c-d45715662c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220063} :answer-field/id #uuid "54f581fb-cfba-5cbd-a01f-835c4dd46f85" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220061} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047a2097-7001-519d-9258-13881780ad45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220065} {:answer/id #uuid "1ce33213-86e8-5d41-a454-4b00318bea8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ce33213-86e8-5d41-a454-4b00318bea8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220066} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe73cb75-70db-5bad-a4ce-7d9e51725207" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220064} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69b6f986-a9fe-5f11-9e0b-38ab9a865d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220068} {:answer/id #uuid "700e9881-8893-5bb6-bc69-a63745dc391e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "700e9881-8893-5bb6-bc69-a63745dc391e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220069} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4535677-c601-5697-b2ea-1317192a2ac9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220067} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11b5888e-1367-56a5-9175-dfea6bb9f319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{b(t),c(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352368} {:answer/id #uuid "513cb401-72ae-50b4-8788-dc72003acf21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a(t),c(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352369} {:answer/id #uuid "74f99df5-e07e-548a-95d3-beda4fbc96ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a(t),b(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "513cb401-72ae-50b4-8788-dc72003acf21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a(t),c(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352369} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f68777d-5b85-570d-849a-2f90bd507d97" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352367} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eaf32a15-4787-500c-be78-eca50b8b0b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b,c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352372} {:answer/id #uuid "1712f948-15f9-566b-986b-7b8eb4025ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a,b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352373} {:answer/id #uuid "389a60c2-3485-5ce8-8572-37eff79f1ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a,c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "389a60c2-3485-5ce8-8572-37eff79f1ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a,c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352374} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6612a91-7ed9-5f28-bac3-2562c8ce6fa1" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352371} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6604d079-1fa4-5548-a15b-700490d300e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352376} {:answer/id #uuid "bd99a357-3b80-5d1b-b3ba-d7c80c32979a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352377} {:answer/id #uuid "b1db3204-9c97-5ded-b301-c8cc04a9a767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352378} {:answer/id #uuid "13d9e56f-bde9-5f48-868e-3d246b8a8970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13d9e56f-bde9-5f48-868e-3d246b8a8970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352379} :answer-field/id #uuid "05127a3c-f46d-5057-9a05-d33b50bcc822" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "611cf62a-767c-5a58-9e5c-4ad812d2faf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-322737" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-6{x}_{1}(t)+7{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below. $\\,$ The function $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-1}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-2}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-3}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}} \nis a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for at least one $t\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(7{e}^{-4t})^{′} \\\\ (2{e}^{-4t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-28{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot 7{e}^{-4t}+7\\cdot 2{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -2\\cdot 7{e}^{-4t}+3\\cdot 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-28{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-28{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ (-2{e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot {e}^{t}+7\\cdot (-2{e}^{t}) \\\\ -2\\cdot {e}^{t}+3\\cdot (-2{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-20{e}^{t} \\\\ -8{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot {e}^{t}+7\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\ -2\\cdot {e}^{t}+3\\cdot {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,c}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,c) & =|\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(7{e}^{-4t})\\cdot {e}^{t}-{e}^{t}\\cdot 2{e}^{-4t} \\\\  & =7{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =5{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $c$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R,$ ${a,c}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b4d9d68-59b6-5209-8265-e1e062c422bf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3220c96-5862-5f94-82ed-83c5018994f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3220c96-5862-5f94-82ed-83c5018994f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352380} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0dd91dd-30c0-5789-9f3d-6a0aac1d0583" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7bfec16a-0969-50de-9ad7-04282efcdd7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-333695" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{t-t} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b4d9d68-59b6-5209-8265-e1e062c422bf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5929aa8-1ec4-5434-86d0-a4da0128686f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5929aa8-1ec4-5434-86d0-a4da0128686f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352383} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0ecbd4b-860d-55f4-aa0d-dbdbba9ee788" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d193e39-56b8-5f6e-821e-907d61fea31a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333696" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2{e}^{3t}\\cdot 5{e}^{-2t}-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =10{e}^{3t-2t} \\\\  & =10{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b4d9d68-59b6-5209-8265-e1e062c422bf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b5cb571-dec9-5e2b-88f4-e25b2de5e68c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352386} {:answer/id #uuid "4d680a26-1b66-5871-9164-3054597dcda9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b5cb571-dec9-5e2b-88f4-e25b2de5e68c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352386} :answer-field/id #uuid "8168ad25-49f4-5d4f-bf53-1d8be92323af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f53fdfca-0e3d-56d4-a5f5-6d1de3cc44e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-333704" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+1 & t \\\\ t & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 1-t\\cdot t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b4d9d68-59b6-5209-8265-e1e062c422bf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70bb9d8d-b1b0-5cfb-be12-b00397021225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352390} {:answer/id #uuid "546c2a08-8373-58c3-abc8-d25ab8e85f12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70bb9d8d-b1b0-5cfb-be12-b00397021225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352390} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac8f17d9-4e3c-5590-8ff2-6a3a83746548" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2de35bb-a108-571f-91e9-0b0c6ae84ae3" :question/math-academy-id "q-333728" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t+1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}t+1 & t-1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(t+1)\\cdot 2-(t-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2t+2-2t+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:kn