) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\cdot (15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))-(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\cdot (3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac674e3f-ac2b-5512-b4fa-9840c6cbd034" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions to Systems of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ebc8c8c-9b65-5c25-bf95-5f424654f2f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352430} {:answer/id #uuid "4efdaf34-187e-599d-aa2f-03974e5eba31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4efdaf34-187e-599d-aa2f-03974e5eba31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352431} :answer-field/id #uuid "