 #edb/ref 17592186352441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "419f4239-71ea-5517-b6f9-5d621b534346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a(t),b(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352441} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dfdd07f-9e79-5842-b92e-ef2c81f2d3b4" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352438} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20aef9a0-b55e-5b8b-838f-52ab76f1f4ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b,c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352443} {:answer/id #uuid "05e27f29-a644-58fe-b3de-0f64c484f1cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a,b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352444} {:answer/id #uuid "c2badceb-c454-546f-975b-b1eff64daee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a,c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05e27f29-a644-58fe-b3de-0f64c484f1cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a,b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352444} :answer-field/id #uuid "923c044f-c95e-538e-ba62-61a2edf5f309" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352442} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b57fb01e-94c9-5512-b5d7-3d95494d45a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{e}^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352447} {:answer/id #uuid "984d0bd1-1c69-57c3-9083-23da10157fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352448} {:answer/id #uuid "a4633777-8d9e-57d9-8b6d-8fde713a46e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352449} {:answer/id #uuid "8d2d50e9-70b0-5532-82d8-87d996829f66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d2d50e9-70b0-5532-82d8-87d996829f66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352450} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8d79f31-fa0d-5506-ace4-5ab4ea687fc6" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61330a4f-0538-5700-9da8-2455820c0258" :question/math-academy-id "q-334527" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The function $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-3t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}]$  \n{{field-1}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2(1-3t) \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}]$  \n{{field-2}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]$  \n{{field-3}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}}  \ndoes not give a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian  \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for all $t\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent. In this case, they do **not** form a fundamental set of solutions.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-3t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(1-3t)^{′} \\\\ (3t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-3t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3(1-3t)+(-3)\\cdot 3t \\\\ 3(1-3t)+3\\cdot 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2(1-3t) \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ also a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(2(1-3t))^{′} \\\\ (6t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2(1-3t) \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot 2(1-3t)+(-3)\\cdot 6t \\\\ 3\\cdot 2(1-3t)+3\\cdot 6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(t)^{′} \\\\ (t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3t+(-3)t \\\\ 3t+3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6t \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,b}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,b) & =|\\begin{aligned}1-3t & 2(1-3t) \\\\ 3t & 6t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(1-3t)\\cdot 6t-2(1-3t)\\cdot 3t \\\\  & =6t(1-3t)-6t(1-3t) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is zero for all $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $b$ are both solutions of the same linear system, but their Wronskian is identically zero, they are linearly dependent and do **not** form a fundamental set of solutions of the system."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee461d3e-3afa-51c6-bddd-355668cac3ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b169f80-81cc-536d-b0b6-b2d7672c44b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10{e}^{6t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352453} {:answer/id #uuid "c476cd61-f104-5b79-8677-83f96e8c362f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352454} {:answer/id #uuid "d5153b3d-0967-5b43-8df8-bd50cd4c8434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{e}^{6t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352455} {:answer/id #uuid "e1492d5d-7f9a-5f0e-ba4e-2e45604c2cd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1492d5d-7f9a-5f0e-ba4e-2e45604c2cd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352456} :answer-field/id #uuid "467c5c5e-c735-5b96-80e5-3b234948d603" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352452} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12b1bf3e-f8e1-5972-81b6-ccdb47646be5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $t\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352458} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f6e723-d624-5b4b-8b28-5b439496ff5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $t\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3f6e723-d624-5b4b-8b28-5b439496ff5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $t\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352459} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed864ed3-7cfb-5f6b-9ee8-480b74fbdbe9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352457} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7f9a3de-049a-521d-bc03-ec3d92587a07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352461} {:answer/id #uuid "86a9cf1a-8457-568c-a7eb-1ee851678de3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7f9a3de-049a-521d-bc03-ec3d92587a07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352461} :answer-field/id #uuid "f04d1102-b0e0-53e9-80d3-27b9ebcaf664" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3aa6e04c-c502-538f-a2a5-b3ef476e8423" :question/math-academy-id "q-352424" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -10{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -10{e}^{3t} & 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(5{e}^{3t})\\cdot (2{e}^{3t})-(-{e}^{3t})\\cdot (-10{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =10{e}^{6t}-10{e}^{6t} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"}]}
